WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
Prisoners of War, Explosive Arcade Action.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:27.160
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.660
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:36.680
And I'm Joe. Guys, SNK back at it again with another arcade port, aka their bread and butter.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.940
But we haven't seen them in a minute.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.500
The last game they released was Baseball Stars.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.800
I'll remind you, an essential game.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.680
Yeah. You know, I actually kind of needed you to, I was going to ask you to tell me which one it was,

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.760
but it's just because I don't really associate SNK with sports games.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.980
So this seems more on brand.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.100
Yeah, I totally forgot that we had an essential sports game.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:13.600
Yeah, we have three now, if you're counting, you know, between Baseball Stars, Tecmo Bowl, and if you count, Mike Tyson's Punch-Out as a sports game.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.120
Okay. Yeah, well, it's Mike Tyson's Punch-Out, I remembered.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.540
I actually also forgot Tecmo Bowl was essential.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.500
Maybe you have an agenda.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.840
I tune those episodes out.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.020
Yeah. SNK also so far on the NES has released Alpha Mission.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.680
I dare you to tell me one thing about that game.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:38.300
It was about guns and shooting, likely, and there was definitely a hero.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:48.880
Okay, good call. Wasn't that the one that was also known as ASO, Armored Scrum Object in Japan, is a vertically scrolling shooter developed by ASO for arcades in 1985?

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.260
You're so right, and yeah, that's correct.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:54.320
It was a shmup.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.500
So, Sean, you could have just taken the easy guess and just guessed shmup.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:06.260
Sure. And that would have been enough for me, but I appreciate the extra effort, because you could argue that every ship in a shmup is the hero.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.200
Yeah, in somebody's life.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:14.520
Right. And then they also released, what do we have here, Iron Tank, The Invasion of Normandy.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.400
That was a cool game where you played in the tank.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.460
Oh, yeah. That was cool.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:19.340
Kind of like the tank controls.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.140
Yeah. Fun stuff.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.260
Lee Trevino's Fighting Golf.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.520
Oh, yeah. Classic.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:26.640
Classic Lee Trevino.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:35.720
And then, just because I'm listing them all off, the only one that remains, Guerrilla War, which is, in a lot of ways, kind of similar to POW.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.000
Yeah. It's modern warfare, too.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.400
There you go. Yeah.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:46.560
I mean, that was more of a run-and-gun, and this is a beat-em-up.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.220
But, you know, the same style.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:55.980
If you remember that one, Sean, that was the one that had, like, you play as Fidel Castro in the non-NES version.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.920
Oh, yeah. I forgot.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:08.420
Yeah. We did make a big deal of all of the political commentary that was just removed because Nintendo had foresight.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:13.000
And the biggest thing just being that SNK, all of these games are arcade games as well.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.600
So, it's just, they're not making original games for the NES.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:25.080
They are just bringing over whatever's selling well in the arcades, which is, honestly, probably the best strategy by a developer we've seen so far.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.640
Right? If it's putting quarters in it, it's probably worth making a home console version of it, too.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.700
You don't even have to really advertise as much.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.480
Everybody already knows what the game is, and they probably think it's just as good as in the arcades before they buy it.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.700
So, you know, you already got a consumer base.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.220
You cannot go wrong.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.060
We've had a bunch of games where you rescue POWs.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.180
Now you finally play as one.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:51.940
Yeah, where are the rescuers?

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.460
No. Why don't we get to just sit here now while another player rescues us?

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.780
We're not some kind of, like, regular old POW.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:07.120
That's true. We save ourselves because they didn't check for explosives, if you remember.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.140
I don't remember from the beginning of the game.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.360
I do remember from the beginning of the game.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:17.580
It does have some cute cut scenes here and there, but just to jump right into the game, why not?

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.240
I think we've done enough SNK backstory.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:21.480
POW, Prisoners of War.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.200
I don't know if you have to say both.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.880
I feel like you could just say one or the other.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:26.960
I'll probably just say POW from this point onwards.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.220
It's a beat-em-up game that takes place during the Cold War.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.480
Players control the POW who has broken free from their cell

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.980
to fight their way through the main base and eliminate the person who,

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.800
or I guess, like, the person who's in charge of the whole war, right?

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:47.240
It's one thing to be like, this guy is, you know, the one who locked me up,

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.320
but it sounds like you're, like, now going to escape jail

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.720
and then take down an entire country.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.920
That is probably the funniest phrase.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.740
Like, I'm here to take down the person responsible for the entire war.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.720
Like, who's the manager of this war?

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.680
I'm going to take him down.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.500
I would like to speak to the manager of this.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.020
He really is the Karen.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.200
Yeah, that's true. I was going to say, I think that it makes you wonder

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.300
if maybe him becoming a prisoner of war was part of his plan.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.720
Like, that was, like, just to get him on the inside,

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.100
and then he breaks down. Oh, it was.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:22.840
Yeah. Is that canon?

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:28.300
That's canon. If you let the start screen just linger for a bit,

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.540
you get a PowerPoint presentation,

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:35.380
like an overhead projector has, like, some text,

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.420
and there's, like, a guy that's describing, like,

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:38.520
you're going to be a POW.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:39.780
You're going to do this.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:41.360
Well, there you go.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:42.800
I played this game.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.700
Yeah. That's great.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:46.900
It's some movie trailer stuff, though.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.160
It is cool. Like, you know, just one problem.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:50.840
He wanted to be caught.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.700
I love that he does have, like, I'm looking at it now.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.240
I love that he does have, like, the, uh,

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.960
like, the elementary school overhead projector.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:05:59.960
Exactly. You can see it there.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.000
So the plot of the game sounds pretty intense to me.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.560
How is the game from an intensity point of view?

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.940
Like, you're alone in enemy ground.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:27.380
Is this a relentless onslaught of, like, you taking down enemies?

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:32.460
And, uh, you know, we've seen a lot of other beat-em-ups try to simulate that arcade feeling.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.720
So what did you guys just feel about, I said intensity,

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.520
but really just, like, the combat experience of the game?

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.580
Well, I think it's funny that you, that you start, you led with intensity,

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:47.360
because intensity was sort of, like, the buzzword I had the whole time

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.080
for, like, things I wanted to talk about, about this game.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:54.620
In the sense that, like, yeah, there's always enemies coming at you and whatnot.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.620
Like, you know, there's always an onslaught.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.560
But something about it feels a little more, like,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.500
a little less reaction-based than a lot of other, like, beat-em-ups I've played.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.620
Like, I'm thinking specifically of, like, um,

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:08.520
not the Turtles game we played,

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.020
the other one on the NES I used to play as a kid.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.040
Um, not, not Turtles in Time.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:14.440
Double Dragon? No, no, another Turtles game.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.240
Um. Oh, oh, uh.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:17.460
Not Turtles in Time, the second.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.920
Oh, Turtles in Time. Not Turtles in Time, not the Super Nintendo, whatever.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.120
Turtles in Time as well.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.500
I'm thinking of those types of games where they feel,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.340
they feel, like, very action-y,

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.020
feels very, like, um,

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.320
fast-paced and, like, reaction-based.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.740
Where this, I don't know, it moves a little slower,

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.900
and literally in the way it controls to me.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:38.080
Not necessarily, like, a horrible thing,

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.600
but just, it didn't feel like

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.400
relentless action-packed-ness.

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:45.140
That's interesting that,

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:46.340
that's the way you describe it,

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:47.620
because, um,

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.260
I would say that, um,

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:54.380
compared to the last beat-em-up that we played,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.920
this feels more classically beat-em-up.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:07:59.480
And, you know me,

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:01.220
you know I have a very short memory,

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:02.840
and I can't remember the games that we played,

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:05.500
but I forgot the name of the last beat-em-up that we played.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:08.020
Um, but, like,

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.660
this one actually has, like,

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:11.260
the sort of cadence and rhythm

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:12.500
that I sort of associate

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.100
with the beat-em-up genre,

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.460
and felt more like Turtles in Time

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:20.240
than a lot of the beat-em-ups that we played so far.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.100
The last beat-em-up we played was Bad Street Brawler.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:25.420
And there it is, yes.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.860
And that was, I would agree that this is a step-up,

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:31.520
both in, both in the combat

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.520
and in the intensity,

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.080
like, the amount of enemies that they give you.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:37.120
I think my complaint

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:38.360
is more of just, like,

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.220
a, a graphical one

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:41.120
that I felt like everything

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:42.840
could have just been zoomed in more.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:44.400
There's a lot of empty space

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.420
on the screen, and most of the action just has,

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.040
you know, because it's melee combat,

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:49.180
you don't need, like,

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:50.940
a lot of space between enemies.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:52.780
So there's a lot of, like, walking

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:54.420
to the enemy,

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:55.440
and, you know,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.280
it's not like, they could throw the knife at you,

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:58.260
but then they don't have a knife,

00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:59.200
and some have guns,

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.260
but for the most part, there's very little

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:03.000
to fill that space,

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:04.140
and so I would have just brought everything

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:05.800
in a little closer,

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:06.940
but in terms of,

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.180
you know, the,

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:09.380
the waves of enemies

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:10.940
and stuff like that, it's all,

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:12.540
it's all, like, appropriately spaced.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:13.780
I never felt like I was just like,

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:14.960
all right, more of this,

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:16.180
more of that, you know,

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:17.480
they shake it up every now and then.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.380
Yeah, I, I sort of agree

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:20.400
and sort of disagree. I mean,

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.040
here's where, where I think that,

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:23.340
the reason I think we have

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:25.400
maybe different descriptions

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:26.900
at least Sean and I,

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:29.040
is because I, I think that,

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:31.200
yes, I am, like, comparing this to just

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.360
my general knowledge

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.480
of, of beat-em-ups I've played

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.860
rather than, rather than looking back

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:38.900
at most recent beat-em-ups

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:40.460
we've played here, in which case,

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:41.400
yes, I totally agree

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.900
that this is closer

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:44.160
to Turtles in Time

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.040
than Bad Street Brawler was

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:47.400
or, or some of these other beat-em-ups,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:48.700
but I still wouldn't,

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:49.860
I'm not saying that this is

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.500
what you were saying, but I still wouldn't consider this

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:53.240
close to Turtles in Time

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:57.060
at all, just because I,

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:58.740
I, I did feel like,

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.760
maybe, maybe we did have a different

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.880
perspective on it, but I did feel like this game

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:04.680
just, it feels like,

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:05.880
like a little more like, okay,

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.720
I see this enemy, I know he's about to do this attack

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:09.800
because of his pattern, so I'm going to move out of the way,

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:10.860
and then I see this guy,

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:12.600
and I'm, but it's like, I'm thinking about all that stuff,

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:14.220
I'm, it's, I'm not just reacting,

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:15.780
it's like a little more cerebral

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:17.900
because it felt like it moves

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:19.080
a little slower, or like,

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:20.260
later in the game,

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:21.780
I, I didn't finish it,

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:22.800
but I got pretty far, I got to,

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.580
I think the, pretty close to the last level,

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:26.740
I didn't quite get to it,

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:27.720
but, um,

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.340
later in the game, it's like,

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:30.920
three enemies come on screen,

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.600
and I have to kick them all,

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:35.200
to like, stop them from moving,

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:36.120
as soon as I start kicking them,

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:37.800
they don't move, and I just got to kick them all

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.780
40 times, and then they'll die,

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:40.700
and I was just like,

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:41.780
this doesn't feel like I'm like,

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:42.660
I'm like jumping around,

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:43.860
trying to react,

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:45.720
and like, play a beat-em-up,

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:47.260
it sounds really negative,

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:49.680
but, I think what, what you're describing more is just,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.240
like a, uh,

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.720
like the difference between,

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:55.900
Tecmo Bowl,

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:57.860
feels more like Madden,

00:10:58.020 --> 00:10:59.920
to me, than NFL did,

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:01.840
and, Tecmo Bowl,

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.400
and Madden, have really nothing in common,

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.360
but, in terms of like,

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:07.560
the, uh,

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.480
like the flow of the game,

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:11.240
it, it felt closer to it,

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:12.640
and I guess I'm not saying that,

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:14.740
this is, this is Turtles in Time,

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.000
but it, but it shares a lot more of the,

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.620
the tropes, that I associate with the genre,

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.820
and it's really just more execution,

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.320
that separates them,

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:24.740
and, and I do agree,

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:26.040
with you on like,

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:27.160
the whole like, sort of,

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:29.340
uh, fist sponginess,

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:32.300
of the enemies, but I think that's more like a,

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:34.240
uh, that isn't,

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.120
quite the same kind of difference,

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:37.600
that I would describe.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:39.000
I, I think then, in that case,

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:39.840
I think we're on the same page,

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:41.340
I think we're, yeah, we're just like looking at it,

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:43.340
we're, we're each comparing it to different things,

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:44.700
and then looking at it from that perspective.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.720
You know, beat em ups have this progression,

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.520
of locking the screen,

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:49.900
once you hit a certain point,

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:51.160
until you take out the enemies,

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:53.060
and in Bad Street Brawler,

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:54.980
I saw that as like a real problem,

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:56.180
as, as like the,

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:57.820
the flow of that game.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:11:59.700
Here, I would agree that,

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.340
it takes in, unnecessary amount of hits,

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:03.800
uh, depending on, you know,

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:05.560
if you're just punching the enemies,

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:06.660
it does take a while,

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.360
to knock them out successfully,

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:09.760
but, for the most part,

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:11.360
they don't fill the screen,

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:13.400
with like an unnecessary amount of enemies,

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:15.380
and they find a creative ways,

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.460
to change up the enemies,

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:19.080
between, between levels too,

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.640
so I feel like there is,

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:22.000
from a flow standpoint,

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:23.660
it feels more like,

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:25.200
the arcade style game,

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:27.020
where, you're moving,

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:28.760
but you know, you're moving progressively,

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:30.660
and things are getting harder,

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:32.120
the stakes are getting bigger,

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:35.140
rather than just, and here's three more green guys,

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:36.900
and then here's three pink guys,

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:38.160
and then here's three blue guys,

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:39.200
you know what I mean? Yeah,

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:41.240
it's like, it's like when we talk about platformers,

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.160
like, are they just throwing enemies anywhere,

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.240
or do they think about,

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:46.340
who should come where, and what's good game design?

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:48.220
The one thing that I would say,

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:50.420
about the whole start and stop,

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:53.580
of it, and the distribution,

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:54.800
of enemies,

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:58.620
